      <h1>Battery Market Overview</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Market Intelligence · Data period: 2024–2026 (varies by source) · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> The battery market splits into a <strong>mature lead-acid segment</strong> — large,
        low-growth, commodity-priced, dominated by Chinese production — and a <strong>fast-growing lithium-ion
        segment</strong> — concentrated in Asia (China, South Korea, Japan) and driven by electric vehicles and energy
        storage. China is the largest producer in both.
      </div>

      <h2>The two segments</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Lead-acid</th><th>Lithium-ion</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Stage</strong></td><td>Mature / commodity</td><td>High growth</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Main demand</strong></td><td>Automotive SLI, industrial, UPS</td><td>EV, energy storage, portable</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Production hub</strong></td><td>China (largest)</td><td>China, South Korea, Japan</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Price character</strong></td><td>Low, commodity</td><td>Falling but technology-differentiated</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Lithium-ion: the Asia concentration</h2>
      <p>
        Lithium-ion cell and pack production is heavily concentrated in Asia. China hosts the largest cell
        manufacturers (CATL, BYD, EVE, CALB), with South Korea (LG Energy Solution, Samsung SDI, SK On) and Japan
        (Panasonic) as the other major producers. In the EV segment, CATL and BYD together account for a large
        share of global installations — figures vary by source and quarter, so this page states the structure rather
        than a single point estimate.
      </p>

      <h2>Lead-acid: China as the production base</h2>
      <p>
        China is the world's largest lead-acid battery producer and exporter. The market is mature and commodity-like,
        with thin margins and large volumes, and it serves automotive starting (SLI), industrial standby and motive
        power. The competitive advantage is scale and cost, not technology differentiation — which is why lead-acid
        is a volume game while lithium is a technology game.
      </p>

      <h2>Why figures vary (read this before trusting any number)</h2>
      <p>
        Market-size estimates for "the battery market" vary widely because sources use different scopes — cells
        versus packs, new versus recycled, automotive versus all applications, and value versus volume. A
        responsible figure must state its <strong>data period, geography, definition and source</strong>. See the
        <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> for how this site handles those conflicts.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> The single most important structural fact about the battery market is the
        Asia concentration — and any market analysis that ignores China's production share in both lead-acid and
        lithium is missing the story.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> Demand, price and supply all flow from the production hub. Understanding where the
        cells and batteries are made — and how concentrated that is — explains more about price trends and supply
        risk than any single market-size figure.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not investment or purchasing advice.</em></p>
      </div>

